>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `SAFE_TAC`

``` hol4
classicalLib.SAFE_TAC : thm list -> tactic
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Repeatedly applies safe classical-reasoning steps to all goals.

`SAFE_TAC thms` starts with the current theory's classical rule set and
uses matching, rather than instantiating variables in the goal. It applies
built-in assumption, contradiction, assumption modus-ponens, implication,
universal-introduction and hypothesis-substitution steps, together with
registered safe introduction, elimination and destruction rules. Registered
safe wrappers also participate. Newly generated goals are rescanned from the
left, and rule-premise order is preserved.

The theorem list can modify the rule set for this invocation. The markers
`SIntro`, `Intro`, `SElim`, `Elim`, `SDest`, `Dest` and `Del` respectively
add or remove rules. Every unmarked theorem is inserted into the goal as an
assumption before safe reasoning starts, so `SAFE_TAC` can use it through
the ordinary assumption channel. Content-bearing generic simplifier
wrappers such as `Once th` are unwrapped and inserted; inert controls such
as `Cong`, `Excl` and `SF` are ignored. No modification survives the tactic
invocation.

The tactic need not solve the goal: successful safe steps may leave
residual goals.

### Failure

After its temporary rule set has been constructed, fails exactly when
saturation leaves the goal unchanged, up to alpha conversion. Use
`TRY (SAFE_TAC thms)` when an inapplicable tactic should succeed without
changing the goal.

Malformed extra rules can also cause failure while the theorem list is
processed.

### Example

The seed rule set proves this propositional theorem without extra rules:

``` hol4
Theorem safe_tac_example:
  p /\ q ==> q /\ p
Proof
  classicalLib.SAFE_TAC []
QED
```

A rule named `my_intro_rule` can be made safe for just one call:

``` hol4
classicalLib.SAFE_TAC [clasetLib.SIntro my_intro_rule]
```

